WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:05.390
Disney's Wreck-It Ralph was a veritable easter
eggstravaganza for video game nerds, especially

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:09.540
those that spent half their lives in the arcade
during the '80s and '90s.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.020
Here's a look at some of the things in the
film only adults will have noticed.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.980
There's a lot going on with the two policemen
characters introduced in the Sugar Rush realm.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.609
The pair serve the tyrannical King Candy,
meaning they're pretty fast and loose with

00:00:21.609 --> 00:00:26.180
their use of tasers and batons, which reads
a bit like a statement in itself, especially

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:30.590
when factoring in the Surge Protector's "random"
screening stops with Ralph.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.240
"Awwww."

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:33.140
"Step aside, sir.

00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:34.210
Random security check."

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:36.320
"Random security check my behind, you always
stop me."

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.640
"I'm just a surge protector doing my job,
sir.”

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.950
Not to mention their motto is "to heat and
serve," which is pretty close to the usual

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.059
law enforcement credo of "to protect and serve."

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:51.910
On a lighter note, they're also shaped like
a donut and an eclair, and are named and Duncan

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.780
as an homage to Winchell's Doughnut House
and Dunkin' Donuts.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.960
To children, they may seem like any of the
other personified edibles running around the

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.399
game, but their parents are sure to get a
chuckle out of this somewhat unsubtle play

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.600
on the whole "cops like donuts" stereotype.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:09.720
Generation Z-ers might be inclined to fixate
on some of the more contemporary references

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.220
scattered about the screen in Wreck-It Ralph,
things like Dance Dance Revolution, Bowser

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.860
from Super Mario Bros. and the Halo-esque
soldiers riffed on in Hero's Duty.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.630
But there are some background characters with
much longer histories.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:27.549
Frogger, Street Fighter, and Sonic the Hedgehog
are probably still mainstream enough to ring

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:29.070
a bell for most people.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.770
Some of the more obscure characters in the
film, however, include Tapper's bowtie-wearing

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:33.770
bartender and.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:38.130
And while fully-grown filmgoers might find
their fingers glued to the pause button as

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.659
they scour the screens for these hidden gems,
the younger crowd probably doesn't care about

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.340
a bunch of 8-bit oldies.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.259
Even the most obvious game reference of all
in Wreck-It Ralph could be easily missed by

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:53.399
anyone who wasn’t around for the glory days
of the earliest arcade systems.

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:58.560
Fix-It Felix is purely a cinematic creation,
but was undoubtedly inspired by Donkey Kong,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.860
which debuted in 1981 on an arcade machine
that also featured the debut of Jumpman, otherwise

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.590
known as Mario.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:10.160
Donkey Kong as a character will still be familiar
to today's device-wielding whippersnappers,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.250
since he appears in modern games like Mario
Kart and Super Smash Bros., so it's probably

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:19.290
not too surprising that his hulking size,
brute strength, and general oafishness inspired

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.480
a lot of Ralph's physical and personality
traits.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.010
It's Fix-It Felix's mission and play style
that really bears a striking resemblance to

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.360
the old Donkey Kong, though.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:38.960
"Anyhoo, what else?

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.240
Uh…

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.240
I'm a wrecker.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.410
I wreck things, professionally."

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:45.959
"I'm gonna wreck it!”

00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:50.230
In both games, the do-gooder is charged with
climbing a large structure while the villain

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.069
tosses debris down at them.

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:55.820
They might not be exactly the same, but, well,
they practically are.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:00.200
It's not until the tail end of Wreck-It Ralph
that audiences find out the film features

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:02.770
another Disney Princess to add to the collection.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:07.920
But even after she's suited up in a ball gown,
Vanellope Von Schweetz handles this fact very

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.940
differently than her forebears.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:14.800
Sure, there are a few theoretical similarities
to the princesses of old: she's been deprived

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.129
of her title by an evildoer, gets bullied
by a bunch of mean girls and is frequently

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:23.019
told that her eccentricities, in this case,
glitches, are troublesome.

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:25.680
So far, so Disney Princess.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.849
Unlike her peers, however, Vanellope has no
interest in wearing the crown.

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:31.060
"I was here.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:31.960
I was there.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.980
I was glitchin' through the walls.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:34.980
I'm not giving that up."

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.150
For the most part, there's nothing stereotypically
princessy about her; she's feisty, resourceful,

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.430
determined and has absolutely no interest
in living a prim life of privilege.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.190
Not only that, but there's nary a love interest
in sight either, and while Ralph does help

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:52.180
her fulfill her dream of earning a spot in
the big race, she does a lot of the legwork

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.180
herself.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.160
The Diet Coke and Mentos geyser gag that saves
the day in Wreck-It Ralph is based on a real

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.500
trend that became big in the mid-aughts after
an on-air demonstration of the effect went

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.850
viral, subsequently inspiring an episode of
MythBusters.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.830
For those who grew up ahead of the internet
age, though, there was a much earlier version

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:13.430
of the messy at-home experiment that everyone
just had to try.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.440
Way back when, it was the Wint-O-Green flavored
Lifesavers that kids would drop into their

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:20.769
soda bottles to set off a massive liquid eruption.

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:24.830
That was just one of the many food-item combination
challenges that would get around by word of

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.370
mouth in the '90s.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:30.440
So while the candy-soda combo trick in Wreck-It
Ralph may be a convenient plot device that

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.440
kids can shrug their shoulders at, the older
crowd might just get a tinge of nostalgia

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.949
after seeing it come into use.

